==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|abjudicate|-ion}} or from {{bor|en|la|abiūdicātiō}}. {{etydate|1623}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əbˌd͡ʒuː.dɪˈkeɪ.ʃən/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-abjudication.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=5}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|law|rare}} {{1|rejection}} or [[confiscation]] by [[judicial]] [[sentence]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1904|title=The Rise of British West Africa|author=Claude George|publisher=Houlston & Sons|page=135|chapter=11|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Rise_of_British_West_Africa/KTpCAAAAIAAJ|text=However, an appeal lay from the Council against any sentence, '''abjudication''' or removal, to the Court of Directors, within one calendar month from date of such sentence or '''abjudication''', upon the aggrieved party giving security sufficient to cover the cost of the appeal.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1861|title=Liber Albus|author=City of London Corporation|editor=Henry Thomas Riley|compiler=John Carpenter|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Liber_Albus/HRQjAAAAMAA|page=496|text='''Abjudication''' of the freedom of Richard Northbury, and restitution thereof{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A History of Germanic Private Law|author=Rudolf Hübner|author2=Paul Vinogradoff|authorlink2=Paul Vinogradoff|author3=William Emanuel Walz|year=1918|publisher=Little, Brown|page=108|text=In the '''abjudication''' of of all or certain rights of civil honor, decreed as a criminal penalty, one can trace the old "Rechtlosigkeit" due to crime, and the Roman "infamia iuris mediata."}}

